{#
_config_backup_plan.html.twig — what a config backup would do, or has just done.

Accepts:
  plan — App\Domain\DTO\Backup\ConfigBackupPlan

Shared by the admin page and the first-run restore screen, because the review is
the substance of an import and two copies of it would be two accounts of the
same file. The pages around it differ; this does not.

The proportions between the lists are the point. What plMail writes itself is
nearly everything. What still needs a person should, on a supported deployment,
be nothing at all — and everything below exists to keep that "nothing" honest
rather than merely small:

  - people this install already had are their own list, because "what did this
    do to my users" deserves a legible answer and the answer is "nothing";
  - the chore list drops values already identical to the live ones, and states
    how many there were in one muted line. A row telling an operator to set
    something to what it is already set to is not a task, and two of them
    appeared on every stock restore;
  - the APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY note is a collapsed disclosure. It matters
    enormously to the operator who is also restoring the old database and not
    at all to anyone else, and it was shouting at both.

This used to be two lists in the other proportion, with two dozen environment
variables printed as lines to paste into .env.local. That was built on a wrong
premise — nobody hand-edits those on a plMail install; they are generated into
var/secrets/generated.env on first run, and the app can write that file. The
restart notice below replaces the whole wall: one sentence for the whole plan,
because it IS one action.

`plan.applied` decides tense throughout: the same list is a promise before the
button and a receipt after it, and a receipt written in the future tense is how
somebody comes to press apply twice.
#}
<div class="space-y-4">

    {# Which file this is. An operator restoring the wrong backup is usually
       holding two, and the only thing telling them apart is what is inside. #}
    <dl class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-xs">
        <dt class="text-ink-faint">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.from'|trans }}</dt>
        <dd class="font-mono text-ink-soft break-all">
            {{ plan.instance ?? ('admin.config_backup.review.unknown_instance'|trans) }}
        </dd>
        <dt class="text-ink-faint">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.exported_at'|trans }}</dt>
        <dd class="font-mono text-ink-soft">
            {{ plan.exportedAt ? plan.exportedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') : ('admin.config_backup.review.unknown_instance'|trans) }}
        </dd>
    </dl>

    {# ── What plMail writes itself ────────────────────────────────────── #}
    <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
            {{ (plan.applied ? 'admin.config_backup.review.written_applied' : 'admin.config_backup.review.written')|trans }}
        </h3>

        {% if plan.written is empty %}
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs text-ink-faint">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.written_none'|trans }}</p>
        {% else %}
            <ul class="mt-2 divide-y divide-line rounded-lg border border-line">
                {% for item in plan.written %}
                    <li class="flex items-center gap-3 px-3.5 py-2">
                        <i class="{{ item.section.icon }} text-xs text-zinc-400 w-4 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft break-all min-w-0">{{ item.key }}</span>

                        {# Two badges, because they answer two different
                           questions: what this does to the value that was here
                           (absent / differs / unchanged), and when it becomes
                           real (now / at the next start). #}
                        {% if item.disposition.needsRestart %}
                            <span class="ml-auto shrink-0 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">
                                {{ 'admin.config_backup.disposition.applied-on-restart'|trans }}
                            </span>
                        {% endif %}

                        <span class="{{ item.disposition.needsRestart ? 'shrink-0' : 'ml-auto shrink-0' }} text-[11px] font-medium px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full
                                     {{ item.change.value == 'differs' ? 'bg-amber-500/15 text-amber-500'
                                        : (item.change.value == 'absent' ? 'bg-emerald-500/15 text-emerald-500'
                                        : 'bg-raised text-ink-faint') }}">
                            {{ item.change.transKey|trans }}
                        </span>
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        {% endif %}

        {# The single instruction that replaced two dozen. Asked once for the
           whole plan, and only when something material is actually waiting on
           it — a re-import that changes nothing has no reason to send anybody
           to a terminal. #}
        {% if plan.needsRestart %}
            <div class="mt-2.5 flex items-start gap-2.5 rounded-lg border border-line bg-raised/40 px-3.5 py-2.5">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-rotate mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-xs text-zinc-400" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
                    {{ (plan.applied ? 'admin.config_backup.review.restart_applied' : 'admin.config_backup.review.restart')|trans }}
                </p>
            </div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {# ── People this install already had ──────────────────────────────── #}
    {% if plan.keptUsers is not empty %}
        <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.kept_users'|trans }}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.kept_users_lead'|trans }}</p>

            <ul class="mt-2 divide-y divide-line rounded-lg border border-line">
                {% for item in plan.keptUsers %}
                    <li class="flex items-center gap-3 px-3.5 py-2">
                        <i class="{{ item.section.icon }} text-xs text-zinc-400 w-4 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft break-all min-w-0">{{ item.key }}</span>
                        <span class="ml-auto shrink-0 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">{{ item.change.transKey|trans }}</span>
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# ── The residue: what still needs a person ───────────────────────────

       plan.chores, not plan.instructed: a restored value already identical to
       the live one is not a task, and printing it in the imperative under
       "these are yours to do" taught readers that this list can be skipped.
       The count of those is stated once, muted, below — see
       ConfigBackupPlan::chores(). #}
    {% set chores = plan.choresBySection %}

    {% if chores is not empty %}
        <div>
            <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.instructed'|trans }}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">{{ 'admin.config_backup.review.instructed_lead'|trans }}</p>

            <div class="mt-2 space-y-3">
                {% for sectionValue, items in chores %}
                    {# Off the first item rather than off the loop key: the key
                       is the enum's backing value and a template that turned it
                       back into a case would be re-implementing from_value. #}
                    {% set firstItem = items|first %}
                    {% set section = firstItem.section %}

                    <div class="rounded-lg border border-line overflow-hidden">
                        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 h-9 border-b border-line bg-raised/40">
                            <i class="{{ section.icon }} text-xs text-zinc-400" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <h4 class="text-xs font-semibold text-ink">{{ section.transKey|trans }}</h4>
                        </div>

                        <ul class="divide-y divide-line">
                            {% for item in items %}
                                <li class="px-3.5 py-2.5">
                                    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                                        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft break-all min-w-0">{{ item.key }}</span>
                                        <span class="ml-auto shrink-0 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">{{ item.change.transKey|trans }}</span>
                                    </div>

                                    {# select-all on the line itself: this is the
                                       thing the operator came for, and a triple
                                       click that catches the label instead of the
                                       value is how a truncated secret ends up in
                                       a compose file. #}
                                    {% if item.instruction %}
                                        <pre class="mt-1.5 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-zinc-500/10 px-2.5 py-1.5 text-[11px] font-mono text-ink-soft select-all whitespace-pre-wrap break-all">{{ item.instruction }}</pre>
                                    {% endif %}

                                    <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink-faint leading-relaxed">{{ item.disposition.transKey|trans }}</p>
                                </li>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {# ── Values that already match ─────────────────────────────────────

       One muted line, never rows. The reader's question about these is "is
       anything hiding in there", and the answer is a number. #}
    {% if plan.alreadyMatching > 0 %}
        <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-faint leading-relaxed">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-check mr-1" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            {{ 'admin.config_backup.review.already_matching'|trans({'%count%': plan.alreadyMatching}) }}
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    {# ── Stated, not asked ────────────────────────────────────────────────

       A native <details>, the same disclosure the settings pages use, because
       this block is addressed to one reader in a hundred: the operator who is
       ALSO restoring the old database and therefore needs its old encryption
       key in their hand. Everyone else was being shown a heading, a sentence
       and a base64 secret about a thing they will never do.

       Collapsed, not removed. An operator who restores the old database
       without its old key loses every encrypted row in it, silently and
       permanently, and there is no second place that tells them so. The
       summary is written to be recognised by the person it is for and ignored
       by everybody else. #}
    {% set keyNote = plan.encryptionKeyNote %}

    {% if keyNote %}
        <details class="rounded-lg border border-line">
            <summary class="flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-2 text-xs text-ink-muted cursor-pointer select-none hover:text-ink transition-colors">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-question text-zinc-400" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                {{ 'admin.config_backup.review.old_key_summary'|trans }}
            </summary>

            <div class="border-t border-line px-3.5 py-2.5">
                <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft break-all">{{ keyNote.key }}</span>
                <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink-faint leading-relaxed">{{ keyNote.disposition.transKey|trans }}</p>

                {% if keyNote.instruction %}
                    <pre class="mt-1.5 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-zinc-500/10 px-2.5 py-1.5 text-[11px] font-mono text-ink-soft select-all whitespace-pre-wrap break-all">{{ keyNote.instruction }}</pre>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        </details>
    {% endif %}
</div>
